= [S2E-models struggle to predict gene expression of genome-wide integrated reporter genes]
The benchmark results show that model predictions are uncorrelated with one group of benchmark measurements, what here call "position effects". Both tasks in that group use a similar setup where the authors insert a fixed gene expression casette, complete with promoter, reporter protein and terminator at different sites in the genome. In one task, the reporter casette is placed at the position of a non-essential gene #footnote[This is the knockout collection where each strain has one non-essential gene replaced by an antibiotic resistance.] and in the other in regions between genes. Both tasks share that the sequence flanking the reporter casette changes. In our benchmark results we observe no Spearman/Pearson correlation between the measurements (reporter fluorescence) and predicted coverage. 

One critique of the measurements and their predictability might be that variance in fluorescence levels might be due to changes in the trans environment and not a consequence of the new cis-environment. #todo[Is this a real possibility? -> counterargument: the authors fit a model that predicts their results from features like distance to next chromatin block]

We suspect from our previous results that models derive their predictions from the coding sequence and are less sensitive to sequence changes in flanking regions. Focussing on one of the benchmarks (Hong; intergenic region insertions) we noticed that predictions for both models have less variance than the ground truth fluorescence (normalized to the same measurement) which would be in line with the hypothesis that current models are not sensitive enough to changes outside of the coding sequence. 

An in-silico saturation mutagenesis shows the sensitivity of model predictions to single nucleotide changes along the sequence axis. One way to approximate the relative importance of the flanking sequence vs. the coding sequence is to compute the variance of expression for SNPs in the flanking regions and coding sequence. However, this would not prove that our model "overfocuses" on the coding sequence, since we don't know the ground truth relative sensitivities. We already know that in Hong et al. changes in the flanking regions lead to more variance than our models predict. To generate another proofpoint of this hypothesis, we generate ISMs for all training sequences and compare the average sensitivity to SNPs in the flanking regions to the average sensitivity in the coding region. #todo[refer to plot where we show the SD of abs attribution scores] We find that both models are significantly more sensitive to non-flanking regions (promoter, CDS, terminator) and that Yorzoi is most sensitive to changes in the coding sequence while Shorkie is equally sensitive in promoter, coding and terminator sequence. 

Hong et al did a separate analysis where they build a XGBoost model to predict the outcome of their position effect experiments and find that the top three most predictive features are what they call _boundary distance_, _genome compact score_ and _nucleosome density_. A short explanation of the features: 

_Boundary distance_: Defined as $"boundary_distance"_i = min_j |"insertion_site"_i - "boundary"_j|$ where $"boundary"_j in B$ where the set of boundaries $B$ is 
$
B = argmin_i {C(i) | i in [L]}
$
$
C(i) = sum_((x,y)) bb(1)[x < i < y]bb(1)[500 <= y - x <= 10,000]
$
where $i$ is any position in the genome for a genome of length $L$. 

This gives rise to a few questions: 
- Are Yorzoi and Shorkie able to capture this metric in theoretically? 
- Do they capture it in practice? 
- Is this feature taken into account to make gene expression predictions?

For starters, we can exclude the hypothesis that this is due to the boundary being too far away: 80% of boundary distances are less than 1 kb away sitting comfortably in the sequence window and less than 5% of boundaries are more than 2 kb away. 

One way to break down whether models have an internal representation of nearest boundary is to ask whether models have (1) a notion of a _boundary_ in the sense of Hsieh et al., (2) a representation of distance of any position to that boundary and (3) a way to represent the minimum of the insertion site position distance to all other in-window boundaries. To measure this, we train linear probes on the outputs of three model blocks, specifically the outputs of the downsampling layer, the transformer blocks and the model trunk (after upsampling, before the linear head).

We train three linear probes on the outputs of (1) the convolution tower, (2) the transformer blocks and (3) the trunk output. Each linear probe is trained to predict the distance to the next boundary for each position in the input sequence. After the convolutional tower the Pearson correlation of predicted and true distance profile is 0.16, increases to 0.46 after the transformer blocks, decreases to 0.31 after the full trunk (before linear output head). The median position in a window is off by 578 bp for the transformer output linear probe. As such, we do not find evidence that it is possible to decode this distance to boundary feature with a linear probe. 

#todo[Two directions to explore the position effect]

From here, there is two natural follow-up questions: 
- Karollus et al. show that the distance of a regulatory element to the gene it regulates is inversely correlated in importance in human sequence-to-function models no matter the biological ground truth.
  - Are we observing the same issue here? 
  - Is this caused by the model not seeing long-range interactions very often or is this caused by the architecture where, in order to compute pairwise interactions, a sequence feature has to be downsampled quite a lot to a coarse representation which might not be as accurate anymore?
- The other approach here is to look at what Hong et al observe to be predictive of their position assay. Then, we can ask if models are in theory able to capture those features and if so why they are not used for making a prediction here.

